WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.980
Command injection prevention is strongest when safe script design is supported by a controlled execution context.

2
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:17.480
A command runs with a current directory, PATH, environment variables, available tools, inherited settings, and the permissions of the identity that launched it.

3
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:22.140
Those assumptions can change behavior even when the command line itself looks careful.

4
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.520
Execution assumptions can change behavior even when the command line itself looks careful.

5
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.240
PATH and command lookup deserve attention.

6
00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.900
For high-impact automation, avoid relying on an uncontrolled PATH.

7
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:43.360
Decide how required tools are located, which versions are expected, and how the script should fail if a dependency is missing.

8
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:48.980
The current working directory should also be explicit when relative paths affect important work.

9
00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.420
Environment variables are inputs.

10
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:59.040
They may influence command lookup, runtime behavior, proxies, credentials, debug settings, and tool configuration.

11
00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:06.580
A safe script knows which environment values it accepts, which ones it sets, and which inherited values should not be trusted blindly.

12
00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:08.700
Least privilege limits impact.

13
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:16.800
A validation mistake or unexpected input path is less damaging when the automation identity has only the access required for the job.

14
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:23.460
Avoid giving broad administrative power to scripts that only need a narrow operational role.

15
00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.840
Permission design is part of command execution safety.

16
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.240
Logging should support operations without exposing secrets.

17
00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:38.340
Avoid printing tokens, private keys, sensitive command output, or full argument lists when they may include confidential values.

18
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:43.400
Good logs explain what the script attempted, what failed, and what an operator should check next.

19
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.760
Review completes the picture.

20
00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.740
ShellCheck can catch many shell-specific mistakes.

21
00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:54.520
Peer review can question design choices, trust boundaries, and operational assumptions.

22
00:01:54.620 --> 00:02:05.820
Realistic tests with empty values, missing files, spaced names, option-like values, and unexpected metadata can reveal problems before automation is trusted in production.
